<h1>Introduction to Basketball</h1>

<p><strong>Basketball</strong> is a fast-paced team sport played by two teams of five players on a rectangular court. Players score points by shooting a ball through a hoop elevated 3.05 m (10 feet) above the floor. Basketball is the third most popular sport globally, with the NBA’s games watched in over 215 countries.</p>

<h2>History of Basketball</h2>

<p>Basketball was invented in <strong>December 1891</strong> by <strong>Dr. James Naismith</strong>, a Canadian physical education instructor working at the International YMCA Training School in Springfield, Massachusetts, USA. Tasked with creating an indoor game to keep students active during the cold winter months, Naismith wrote 13 basic rules and nailed a peach basket to an elevated running track. Players attempted to throw a soccer ball into the basket — and basketball was born.</p>

<p>Key milestones:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>1891</strong> — James Naismith invents basketball with 13 original rules</li>
  <li><strong>1898</strong> — First professional basketball league formed in the USA</li>
  <li><strong>1932</strong> — FIBA (Fédération Internationale de Basketball) founded in Geneva</li>
  <li><strong>1936</strong> — Basketball introduced as an Olympic sport at the Berlin Games</li>
  <li><strong>1946</strong> — NBA (National Basketball Association) founded</li>
  <li><strong>1992</strong> — The USA "Dream Team" (featuring Michael Jordan, Magic Johnson, Larry Bird) wins Olympic gold, boosting global interest massively</li>
  <li><strong>1997</strong> — WNBA (Women’s National Basketball Association) founded</li>
</ul>

<h2>Court Layout and Dimensions</h2>

<table>
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>NBA Dimensions</th><th>FIBA Dimensions</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Court length</td><td>28.65 m (94 ft)</td><td>28 m</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Court width</td><td>15.24 m (50 ft)</td><td>15 m</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Three-point line distance</td><td>7.24 m (23.75 ft at top)</td><td>6.75 m</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Free throw line</td><td>4.57 m from backboard</td><td>4.6 m from backboard</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Basket height</td><td>3.05 m (10 ft)</td><td>3.05 m (10 ft)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Key (paint) width</td><td>4.88 m (16 ft)</td><td>4.9 m</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Key court areas:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The paint (key):</strong> Rectangular area directly below each basket; limits offensive players to 3 seconds inside</li>
  <li><strong>Three-point arc:</strong> Shots beyond this line are worth 3 points</li>
  <li><strong>Free throw line:</strong> Where players shoot free throws after certain fouls</li>
  <li><strong>Half-court line:</strong> Once a team crosses this with the ball, they cannot pass it back (violation = backcourt)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Scoring System</h2>

<table>
  <tr><th>Type of Score</th><th>Points</th><th>How It’s Earned</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Field Goal (inside arc)</td><td>2 points</td><td>Ball enters basket from inside the three-point line</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Three-pointer</td><td>3 points</td><td>Ball enters basket from beyond the three-point arc (shooter’s feet must be behind the line)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Free Throw</td><td>1 point each</td><td>Uncontested shots from the free throw line, awarded after certain fouls</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Player Positions</h2>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Position</th>
    <th>Abbreviation</th>
    <th>Primary Role</th>
    <th>Notable Player</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Point Guard</strong></td>
    <td>PG</td>
    <td>Floor general — organises offense, distributes the ball, pushes pace</td>
    <td>Stephen Curry, Magic Johnson</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Shooting Guard</strong></td>
    <td>SG</td>
    <td>Primary perimeter scorer; often specialises in three-point shooting</td>
    <td>Devin Booker, Michael Jordan</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Small Forward</strong></td>
    <td>SF</td>
    <td>Versatile — scores inside and outside, defends multiple positions</td>
    <td>LeBron James, Kevin Durant</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Power Forward</strong></td>
    <td>PF</td>
    <td>Physical inside scorer; strong rebounder</td>
    <td>Giannis Antetokounmpo, Karl Malone</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Center</strong></td>
    <td>C</td>
    <td>Tallest player; rim protector, interior scorer, sets screens</td>
    <td>Joel Embiid, Shaquille O’Neal</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>Basic Skills</h2>

<h3>Dribbling</h3>
<p>Players must dribble (bounce the ball continuously on the floor) while moving. Key techniques:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Crossover:</strong> Quickly transferring the ball from one hand to the other in front of the body to change direction and beat a defender</li>
  <li><strong>Behind-the-back:</strong> Passing the ball behind the body while dribbling to switch hands while protecting the ball</li>
  <li><strong>Euro step:</strong> Two-step move to avoid a defender while driving to the basket (two steps allowed after gathering the ball)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Passing</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Chest pass</strong> — fast, two-handed pass from the chest; most common in open play</li>
  <li><strong>Bounce pass</strong> — bounced off the floor, useful when defenders have their hands up</li>
  <li><strong>Overhead pass</strong> — two-handed pass above the head; useful for longer distances</li>
  <li><strong>No-look pass</strong> — looking away from the target to deceive defenders</li>
</ul>

<h3>Shooting</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Jump shot:</strong> Player jumps and releases ball at the peak of their jump</li>
  <li><strong>Layup:</strong> Close-range shot using the backboard after approaching the basket; uses two steps</li>
  <li><strong>Slam dunk:</strong> Powerful downward thrust of the ball through the hoop — requires jumping ability</li>
  <li><strong>Free throw:</strong> Stationary shot from the free throw line, released with one or two hands</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rebounding</h3>
<p>Securing possession of the ball after a missed shot. <strong>Offensive rebounds</strong> give the attacking team a second chance; <strong>defensive rebounds</strong> end the opponent’s possession. Boxing out (positioning body between opponent and the basket) is the key technique.</p>

<h2>NBA vs FIBA Rules Comparison</h2>

<table>
  <tr><th>Rule Area</th><th>NBA</th><th>FIBA (International)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Game duration</td><td>4 × 12 min quarters</td><td>4 × 10 min quarters</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Shot clock</td><td>24 seconds (14 on offensive rebound)</td><td>24 seconds (14 on offensive rebound)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Three-point line</td><td>7.24 m (corner: 6.71 m)</td><td>6.75 m uniform</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Restricted area (no-charge zone)</td><td>1.22 m radius</td><td>1.25 m radius</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Team fouls per quarter</td><td>6 (free throws from 7th)</td><td>5 (free throws from 6th)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Goaltending</td><td>Ball cannot be touched once it’s on the way down toward the hoop</td><td>Ball can be touched once it hits the rim (cylinder rule)</td></tr>
</table>

